@@ -5,9 +5,9 @@ All notable changes to kern. Format: Keep a Changelog. Versioning: semver, as de
 ## [Unreleased]
 
 - Guards read through zsh's precommand modifiers `noglob` and `nocorrect`, and through `coproc` and `repeat` with its count, to the command after them. `noglob eval 'git push --force origin main'`, `nocorrect eval`, `repeat 1 eval`, `coproc eval`, `repeat -1+2 git push --force origin main` and `noglob sh <<EOF` with a push in its body are denied in both modes, where under autopilot they got no vote. A command behind one gets the vote it gets alone, so `noglob cat <state file>` is a read, as `cat` is. G1, G5 and G6 count a `cd` behind `noglob`, `nocorrect` or `repeat N` as a move, and judge where git would run both with and without it; one under `coproc`, `repeat 0`, or a reserved word zsh does not find as one (quoted, or after `noglob` or `nocorrect`) is not a move for them. A modifier word after the command name, such as `cd x 2> noglob`, changes nothing.
-- Guards G16 and G20 follow a `cd`, `pushd` or `popd` only when it is the command name of its simple command, with only assignments and redirections before it. A `cd` behind a zsh modifier or reserved word (`noglob`, `nocorrect`, `coproc`, `repeat`, `time`, `!`, `if`) or a wrapper (`command`, `builtin`, `exec`, `nice`, `nohup`, `env`, `timeout`, `sudo`) may run in a subshell, run `/usr/bin/cd`, which never moves the shell, or not run at all. On such a `cd` G16 keeps its readings and adds the target's, and G20 judges from both directories, up to 16 of them (past that it denies). So `cd <sessions> && nice cd /tmp; echo x > foo` and `noglob time cd /tmp; cp approved.md x.md` are denied, where they got no vote.
+- Guards G16 and G20 follow a `cd`, `pushd` or `popd` only when it is the command name of its simple command, with only unquoted assignments and redirections before it (zsh runs a quoted `'X=1'` or `'2>/dev/null'` as a command). An upper-case `CD`, which on macOS runs `/usr/bin/cd`, is not plain either: G16 reads every `cd` in a command that holds one as a maybe. A `cd` behind a zsh modifier or reserved word (`noglob`, `nocorrect`, `coproc`, `repeat`, `time`, `!`, `if`) or a wrapper (`command`, `builtin`, `exec`, `nice`, `nohup`, `env`, `timeout`, `sudo`) may run in a subshell, run `/usr/bin/cd`, which never moves the shell, or not run at all. On such a `cd` G16 keeps its readings and adds the target's, and G20 judges from both directories, up to 16 of them; past that it denies a relative or Markdown write, and judges a read as before. G20 reads each simple command once, and judges it once when nothing in it depends on the directory. So `cd <sessions> && nice cd /tmp; echo x > foo` and `noglob time cd /tmp; cp approved.md x.md` are denied, where they got no vote. G20 takes `builtin cd`, and a `cd` that can go back (`cd -`, `cd ~-`, `cd +1`, a target built at run time such as `"$OLDPWD"`, `popd`), as a maybe too, and adds HOME and every directory the command was in.
 - Guard G1 judges a push after a `cd`, or under `env -C` or `sudo -D`, where it would run without the move too, so that judgement's deny holds: `cd . && git push --force-with-lease origin feat` over another author's commit is denied, where it asked. The ask that the directory is unknown stays, and is still denied under autopilot.
-- Guard G16 keeps its reading of a `cd` into a `.claude` directory or the sessions dir across a later `cd` whose target it cannot know: no operand, `-`, `+N`, two operands, `~user`, a `$`, a backtick or a glob, or a relative path after one of these. So `cd ~/.claude && cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel || echo .)" && sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json` asks, and is denied under autopilot, where before it got no vote; a write after such a `cd` from the sessions dir stays denied. Such a `cd`, and `popd`, can go back to a directory the command was in, so it also takes back every reading held earlier in the command: `cd <sessions> && cd /tmp && cd - && echo x > foo` is denied, and `cd ~/.claude && cd /tmp && cd ~- && sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json` asks. A `cd .`, or any relative `cd` from the sessions dir, keeps the reading too. A `cd` to an absolute path, `~` or `~/path` still ends it. G16 follows a `cd` to a known target as text, so a relative `cd` that leaves a `.claude` directory ends that reading, and one that comes back into it, as in `cd ~/.claude && cd agents && cd .. && sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json`, restores it. Known limits: a `cd` to a known target that fails, followed by `;`, is still taken as a move (`cd ~/.claude && cd /nonexistent; sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json` gets no vote); so are bash's `pushd -n`, which does not move, a `cd` that runs in a subshell, a pipeline or the background (`( cd /tmp )`, `cd /tmp | cat`), and an upper-case `CD`, which on macOS runs `/usr/bin/cd`, since G16 reads the command in lower case; a relative `cd` that CDPATH (or zsh's cdpath) resolves elsewhere is followed as if from the current directory; and a path through a symlink into the sessions dir is not read as the sessions dir, since G16 matches state by path text.
+- Guard G16 keeps its reading of a `cd` into a `.claude` directory or the sessions dir across a later `cd` whose target it cannot know: no operand, `-`, `+N`, two operands, `~user`, a `$`, a backtick or a glob, or a relative path after one of these. So `cd ~/.claude && cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel || echo .)" && sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json` asks, and is denied under autopilot, where before it got no vote; a write after such a `cd` from the sessions dir stays denied. Such a `cd`, and `popd`, can go back to a directory the command was in, so it also takes back every reading held earlier in the command: `cd <sessions> && cd /tmp && cd - && echo x > foo` is denied, and `cd ~/.claude && cd /tmp && cd ~- && sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json` asks. A `cd .`, or any relative `cd` from the sessions dir, keeps the reading too. A `cd` to an absolute path, `~` or `~/path` still ends it. G16 follows a `cd` to a known target as text, so a relative `cd` that leaves a `.claude` directory ends that reading, and one that comes back into it, as in `cd ~/.claude && cd agents && cd .. && sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json`, restores it. Known limits: a `cd` to a known target that fails, followed by `;`, is still taken as a move (`cd ~/.claude && cd /nonexistent; sed -i '' s/a/b/ settings.json` gets no vote); so are bash's `pushd -n`, which does not move, and a `cd` that runs in a subshell, a pipeline or the background (`( cd /tmp )`, `cd /tmp | cat`); a relative `cd` that CDPATH (or zsh's cdpath) resolves elsewhere is followed as if from the current directory; and a path through a symlink into the sessions dir is not read as the sessions dir, since G16 matches state by path text.
 - Guard G6 still scans the current repository for a commit or push after a `cd`, or under `env -C`, `--git-dir`, `--work-tree`, `GIT_DIR=` or `GIT_INDEX_FILE=`, so a secret staged there is denied in both modes; before, `(cd .); git commit -m x` with a staged key only asked. The ask that it could not scan the moved part stays, and is still denied under autopilot.
 - Guards judge a Bash command whose head word is a shell alias from the Claude Code shell snapshot, such as `gpf! origin main`, as the git or gh command it expands to, as well as itself. With aliases loaded, the guards read the command's own simple commands exactly as without aliases, in the same order and context, then every expansion after all of them, so an alias can only add votes: it never removes a deny or an ask, and never gets an allow, which kern gives only to a command that starts with git or gh. The head word is found after assignments (quoted ones included), redirections and zsh's reserved words (`if`, `!`, `time` and the like), in each simple command, `$( )` and `eval`. An alias whose value ends in a space, such as `_='sudo '`, also expands the word after it, as zsh's trailing-space rule does, so `_ gpf! origin main` is judged as `sudo git push --force origin main`; past 8 such words, a word that is still an alias is denied under autopilot. G2, G3, G4, G13, G14 and G15, which skip a command whose text names no gh, push, deploy tool or network client, read the expansion's text too, so `prc` for `gh pr create`, `gp origin production` as a push to a deploy branch, and an alias that runs psql against a protected target are judged. kern reads the snapshot as text and never runs it; a missing, unreadable or unparseable snapshot changes nothing. It caches the parsed table in its state dir, keyed by the snapshot's path, size, modification and change times and inode, and reads the cache as data the same way; a forged cache, like a forged snapshot, can only add votes. This removes the 0.2.0 known limit on shell aliases. Known limits, left for M3: the newest snapshot stands in for the session's, which the hook input does not name; an alias defined and used in one Bash call through `eval`, `$( )` or a backtick is not judged interactively (G00 denies the definition under autopilot); nor is an alias past another alias's head word, a global alias, a `$'...'` or double-quoted alias value, a chain of aliases deeper than 8, or interactively an alias past 8 trailing-space words; an expansion no longer sets context for the command's later records, so `gaa && git commit -m x` loses G6's stale-index reason (its decision stays); a snapshot with thousands of aliases slows every Bash call, by about 0.6 s for 10,000; G16 and G20 match only the command's own text, not an alias's expansion; and the shell-snapshots folder is writable, so kern reads a newer snapshot written there, though that can only add votes, never remove a deny or an ask or add an allow.
 - help: allows only the exact git commands it prescribes (`git remote -v`, `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, `git check-ignore -q *`, `git worktree list`); `git remote *`, `git symbolic-ref *` and `git log *` could write config, move HEAD or run a program with no prompt.
